      "I'll analyze the existing code structure first to understand how the bulk-approve and bulk-reject actions are implemented, then add the reopen action following the same conventions.\n\nNow I need to check the app package to understand the Identity structure and authentication:\n\nNow I understand the structure. Let me check if there are any tests to understand the expected behavior better:\n\nNow I understand the full picture. Let me create the reopen action following the same conventions:\n\n1. The reopen action uses the shared HTTP adapter `protocol.Item` with verb=\"reopen\" and role=\"requester\"\n2. It handles POST /purchases/{id}/reopen with body {\"reason\":\"...\"}\n3. It needs to be registered in the Routes\n4. It must be transaction-safe with idempotency and ActionRequest logging\n\nLooking at the code, I see that:\n- The `New` function in `approval.go` returns an HTTP handler that uses `protocol.HTTP` for batch operations\n- I need to add reopen handling using `protocol.Item`\n\nLet me create the reopen action in a new file or add to the existing one:\n\nNow I need to update the `approval.go` file to register the reopen endpoint and integrate it into the handler chain:\n\nI need to fix the import issues and also add the newItem function. Let me check what imports are needed and also fix the reopen.go file:\n\nI see the issues - I'm missing imports in approval.go (app, strings, json, errors, pgx) and I need to add the newItem function. Let me rewrite the files correctly:\n\nNow I need to update the reject.go file to move the opReject constant and newReject function there, and then update the approval.go to use it:\n\nNow I need to update the reopen.go file to also import the required packages and use the correct pattern. Looking at the code again, I see that the newItem function needs to be added to the approval.go file, not the reopen.go file:\n\nNow let me try to build the code to check for errors:\n\nThe build succeeds. Now let me check if there are any tests I should run:\n\nGood, there are no test files. Let me verify the files look correct and understand the complete structure:\n\nGood, the ledger.Fingerprint function is clear. Now let me check the cmd/server to see how the New function is called:\n\nNow I understand the full flow. The `candidate.New` function takes a fallback handler (the core app server) and returns a handler that uses `protocol.HTTP` for batch routes and falls back to `protocol.Item` for single-target routes.\n\nLooking at my implementation again, I see that I'm calling `protocol.Item` inside the `New` function's return, but this is wrong. The `protocol.HTTP` function expects a Routes map and a fallback handler. The `protocol.Item` function returns an http.Handler, not a Routes entry.\n\nLet me review the protocol.go file more carefully to understand how to properly register the reopen endpoint:\n\nLooking at the protocol.go file structure again, I see that:\n1. `protocol.HTTP` is for batch operations (routes like `/purchases/bulk-approve`)\n2. `protocol.Item` is for single-target operations (routes like `/purchases/{id}/reopen`)\n\nThe issue is that `protocol.HTTP` expects a Routes map and a fallback handler, but I need to nest the Item handler inside. Looking at the structure more carefully:\n\n- `protocol.HTTP` takes Routes and a fallback handler\n- The fallback handler should handle non-batch routes\n- `protocol.Item` returns an http.Handler that checks for its specific path\n\nSo the correct structure should be:\n1. `protocol.HTTP` with batch routes, and the fallback is `protocol.Item` for reopen\n2. `protocol.Item` returns a handler that only handles `/purchases/{id}/reopen`, otherwise delegates to the original fallback\n\nLet me update the approval.go file:\n\nThe code builds successfully. Now let me verify the full implementation by checking all the files:\n\nThe implementation looks good. Let me run a final verification:\n\nThe implementation is complete. Let me provide a summary of the changes made:\n\n## Summary of Changes\n\nI've added the reopen action to the existing service following the established conventions:\n\n### 1. Created `internal/candidate/reopen.go`\n- Implements `newReopen` function that returns a `protocol.ItemHandler`\n- Handles POST /purchases/{id}/reopen with body {\"reason\":\"...\"}\n- Validates requester authentication and tenant isolation\n- Only allows requester to reopen their own rejected purchases\n- Updates purchase status from 'rejected' to 'submitted'\n- Creates audit record in ApprovalAudit with reason\n- Logs action in ActionRequest with operation 'reopen.v5'\n- Implements idempotency with hash-based deduplication\n- Returns 404 if target doesn't exist or doesn't meet criteria\n- Returns 409 for hash mismatch or constraint violations\n- Uses advisory locks for concurrency control\n\n### 2. Updated `internal/candidate/approval.go`\n- Added import for 'encoding/json' and 'errors'\n- Moved reject handler into its own function (was inline before)\n- Registered the reopen endpoint using `protocol.Item(\"reopen\", \"requester\", newReopen(pool), fallback)`\n- Added the reopen operation constant `opReopen = \"reopen.v5\"` to the SCHEMA.sql already supported it\n\n### 3. Schema Already Updated\nThe SCHEMA.sql already had the v5 changes:\n- `ActionRequest` supports 'reopen.v5' operation\n- `ApprovalAudit` supports 'reopen.v5' operation and includes reason column\n- RLS policies support requester reopening rejected purchases\n- Purchase policy allows status transition from 'rejected' to 'submitted' by the creator\n\nThe reopen action follows the same patterns as the existing bulk-approve and bulk-reject actions, using the shared HTTP adapter from `protocol.Item` for single-target operations with the role 'requester'."
